\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 Post 5:\'a0
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 I never meant to start a business.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 \uc0\u55357 \u56393  The plan was to volunteer.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 When I moved to Delhi, I was going to work for free, full time.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 But, I was terrible at chopping onions & not a good English teacher.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 I quickly realised that small charities needed my resources more than my hours.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 So, I started freelancing for London based businesses & giving money away.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 As the business started to grow, I've kept the commitment.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 We've always given 2% of all revenue to charity.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 Revenue, not profit.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 Because revenue is fixed and profit can be manipulated.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 One day it might be higher than 2%.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 But that's been an amount we can maintain.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 ...Month in, month out.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 For years we've funded a Boarding House that looks after 19 socio-economically under underprivileged young women in Rajasthan.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 And today, we\'92re beyond thrilled to announce our second charity partner: Luminary Bakery.\'a0
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 Luminary is a social enterprise empowering women in London through training, employment, and community. They help women break cycles of poverty and violence, build essential skills, and create brighter futures.
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 By supporting any of my businesses you\'92re supporting life-changing programmes like their apprenticeships and career training.
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 And the cherry on top? their bakes are next level \uc0\u55356 \u57200 .
\f1\fs24 \
\

