\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 Pregnancy at work is a taboo no one prepared me for. It needs to change.
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 We talk endlessly about the challenges of returning to work after baby. But NOT about the brutal reality of working while pregnant.
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 No one warned me that:
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 1. "Morning" sickness meant all-day nausea for months
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 2. Commuting would feel like running a marathon
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 3. Baby brain meant using wrong words mid-presentation
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 The expectation to perform at 100% while your body is literally building another human is absurd.
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 Most workplaces have no policies beyond the bare minimum legal requirements. No accommodations for debilitating symptoms that come and go unpredictably.
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 This isn't about special treatment. It's about acknowledging biological reality.
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 The current approach forces pregnant women to either:
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 1. Pretend everything is fine (impossible)
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 2. Appear "weak" or "uncommitted" (career damaging)
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 We need to normalise this conversation. Pregnancy isn't an illness, but it is a physical state that affects performance in ways beyond our control.
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 I'm ready for motherhood. I wasn't ready for pregnancy at work.
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 Here's Lottie's most viral post ever which would be great to take inspiration from:
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 M&S just disrupted the breakfast cereal market with a single ingredient.
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 Corn.
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 That's it. The entire ingredients list on their new Cornflakes.
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 No sugar. No salt. No additives. No preservatives.
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 This isn't just another product launch \'96 it's a direct challenge to the entire industry that's spent decades convincing us that "low calorie" and artificial sweeteners were the path to health.
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 The wellness pendulum is swinging hard toward simplicity and natural ingredients. Away from chemical concoctions masquerading as "health foods."
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 Influence from voices like Eddie Abbew and Tonic Health has been instrumental in this shift, pushing back against the artificial sweetener obsession and championing real, whole foods.
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 M&S isn't just following the trend \'96 they're accelerating it.
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 Smart brands are taking note: consumers increasingly want ingredients they recognize, not chemical compounds they need a science degree to understand.
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 The future of food isn't more complexity \'96 it's radical simplicity.
\f1\fs24 \
\

\f0\fs29\fsmilli14667 Who else is here for the ingredient list revolution?
\f1\fs24 \
